1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,978
A leaf pressed flat inside an old book.

2
00:00:02,978 --> 00:00:04,883
A frog kept in a glass jar.

3
00:00:04,883 --> 00:00:07,373
A tree with a name board in a garden.

4
00:00:07,773 --> 00:00:09,003
Three odd things.

5
00:00:09,003 --> 00:00:12,596
But all three help scientists
name living things correctly.

6
00:00:13,046 --> 00:00:17,539
Diya found a dried leaf between the
pages of her grandfather's old book.

7
00:00:17,539 --> 00:00:21,226
She asked: why would anyone
keep a dead leaf for years?

8
00:00:21,576 --> 00:00:24,889
Scientists keep huge collections
like this on purpose.

9
00:00:24,889 --> 00:00:29,321
Today you meet these tools, the
taxonomic aids, and learn to use a key

10
00:00:29,321 --> 00:00:30,221
yourself.

11
00:00:30,705 --> 00:00:32,782
So what is a taxonomic aid?

12
00:00:33,132 --> 00:00:36,757
To classify a living thing,
you first have to identify it.

13
00:00:36,757 --> 00:00:39,808
You compare it with things
that are already named.

14
00:00:40,258 --> 00:00:44,651
Taxonomic aids are the collections
and records that make this comparison

15
00:00:44,651 --> 00:00:45,551
possible.

16
00:00:45,875 --> 00:00:47,743
There are five you must know.

17
00:00:47,743 --> 00:00:52,060
The herbarium, the botanical garden,
the museum, the zoological park, and

18
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:52,960
the key.

19
00:00:53,408 --> 00:00:56,146
Let's start with the
herbarium, and build one sheet.

20
00:00:56,496 --> 00:00:59,420
A herbarium is a store
house of plant specimens.

21
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:02,583
Each one is dried, pressed,
and kept on a sheet.

22
00:01:03,083 --> 00:01:04,304
Here is one sheet.

23
00:01:04,304 --> 00:01:08,468
The pressed plant is fixed on stiff
paper, with its leaves, stem, and a

24
00:01:08,468 --> 00:01:09,368
flower.

25
00:01:09,548 --> 00:01:11,413
In the corner sits the label.

26
00:01:11,413 --> 00:01:15,606
It carries the date and the place of
collection, and the plant's English,

27
00:01:15,606 --> 00:01:17,227
local and botanical names.

28
00:01:17,727 --> 00:01:21,519
It also gives the family, and the
name of the person who collected it.

29
00:01:22,019 --> 00:01:26,739
Then the sheets are stored in cupboards,
arranged in a system of classification

30
00:01:26,739 --> 00:01:28,857
that scientists everywhere accept.

31
00:01:29,507 --> 00:01:32,962
Now watch how one sheet
is made, stage by stage.

32
00:01:33,312 --> 00:01:34,212
Collect.

33
00:01:33,983 --> 00:01:38,209
A healthy plant part is picked,
with leaves and flowers if possible.

34
00:01:38,959 --> 00:01:39,859
Press.

35
00:01:39,553 --> 00:01:42,960
It is laid flat between
sheets of paper and squeezed.

36
00:01:43,710 --> 00:01:44,610
Dry.

37
00:01:44,219 --> 00:01:48,237
The paper soaks up the water,
until the plant is fully dry.

38
00:01:48,987 --> 00:01:49,887
Mount.

39
00:01:49,569 --> 00:01:52,425
The dry plant is fixed
on a herbarium sheet.

40
00:01:53,175 --> 00:01:54,267
Label and store.

41
00:01:54,267 --> 00:01:57,781
The label is filled in, and
the sheet joins the collection.

42
00:01:58,531 --> 00:02:01,910
Next, three more places
that hold real specimens.

43
00:02:02,260 --> 00:02:05,698
A botanical garden grows
living plants for reference.

44
00:02:05,698 --> 00:02:10,040
Each plant carries a label with
its botanical name and its family.

45
00:02:10,490 --> 00:02:15,236
Famous ones are the Royal Botanical
Garden at Kew in England, the Indian

46
00:02:15,236 --> 00:02:19,846
Botanical Garden at Howrah, and the
National Botanical Research Institute

47
00:02:19,846 --> 00:02:20,810
in Lucknow.

48
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:23,587
A museum keeps preserved specimens.

49
00:02:23,587 --> 00:02:26,188
Some sit in jars of preserving liquid.

50
00:02:26,188 --> 00:02:28,576
Insects are pinned in insect boxes.

51
00:02:28,576 --> 00:02:32,472
Birds and mammals are stuffed,
and skeletons are kept too.

52
00:02:32,922 --> 00:02:37,470
A zoological park keeps wild animals
in a protected place, close to their

53
00:02:37,470 --> 00:02:38,483
natural home.

54
00:02:38,483 --> 00:02:41,492
Here we learn their food
habits and behaviour.

55
00:02:42,142 --> 00:02:44,114
Two of these are easy to mix up.

56
00:02:44,464 --> 00:02:48,832
A herbarium holds dead plants:
dried, pressed, and kept on sheets.

57
00:02:49,282 --> 00:02:53,528
A botanical garden holds living plants,
growing in the ground, each with

58
00:02:53,528 --> 00:02:54,428
its label.

59
00:02:54,786 --> 00:02:55,686
Your turn.

60
00:02:55,673 --> 00:03:00,250
A teacher wants students to watch one
tree flower and fruit over a whole

61
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:01,150
year.

62
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:03,002
Herbarium, or botanical garden?

63
00:03:03,002 --> 00:03:04,306
Pause and decide.

64
00:03:07,506 --> 00:03:08,762
Botanical garden!

65
00:03:08,762 --> 00:03:11,624
Only a living plant can flower and fruit.

66
00:03:11,624 --> 00:03:13,837
A herbarium sheet never changes.

67
00:03:14,487 --> 00:03:18,133
Now the fifth aid, and the
one you can use with a pencil.

68
00:03:18,133 --> 00:03:19,033
The key.

69
00:03:19,188 --> 00:03:23,953
A key helps you identify an organism
by answering pairs of choices.

70
00:03:24,403 --> 00:03:26,709
Each pair shows two opposite features.

71
00:03:26,709 --> 00:03:28,648
This pair is called a couplet.

72
00:03:29,098 --> 00:03:31,928
Each single statement in
the pair is called a lead.

73
00:03:31,928 --> 00:03:35,973
You pick one lead, and it sends you
to the next couplet, or gives you the

74
00:03:35,973 --> 00:03:36,873
name.

75
00:03:37,089 --> 00:03:38,174
Remember this.

76
00:03:38,174 --> 00:03:41,618
A key always offers two
opposite choices at a time.

77
00:03:42,218 --> 00:03:43,812
Now, a worked example.

78
00:03:43,812 --> 00:03:48,865
Let's run four animals through a key:
a crow, a cat, a frog, and a housefly.

79
00:03:49,215 --> 00:03:50,132
Couplet one.

80
00:03:50,132 --> 00:03:52,306
Backbone present, or no backbone?

81
00:03:52,306 --> 00:03:55,416
The housefly has no
backbone, so it leaves here.

82
00:03:55,866 --> 00:03:56,766
Couplet two.

83
00:03:56,723 --> 00:03:59,301
Body covered with
feathers, or no feathers?

84
00:03:59,301 --> 00:04:01,843
The crow has feathers, so it leaves here.

85
00:04:02,293 --> 00:04:03,345
Couplet three.

86
00:04:03,345 --> 00:04:06,727
Body covered with hair, or
moist skin without hair?

87
00:04:06,727 --> 00:04:08,045
The cat has hair.

88
00:04:08,045 --> 00:04:09,765
The frog has moist skin.

89
00:04:10,215 --> 00:04:13,043
Three couplets, four animals, four names.

90
00:04:13,043 --> 00:04:16,296
Every step was one clear
choice between two leads.

91
00:04:16,946 --> 00:04:18,412
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:04:18,762 --> 00:04:21,745
Many students call each
statement in a key a couplet.

93
00:04:22,145 --> 00:04:24,495
But each single statement is a lead.

94
00:04:24,495 --> 00:04:27,340
A couplet is the pair
of two opposite leads.

95
00:04:27,990 --> 00:04:29,443
Where is this asked?

96
00:04:29,443 --> 00:04:32,310
This part is not in
the current NCERT text.

97
00:04:32,310 --> 00:04:36,825
If it comes up, expect statement
questions on the herbarium label, or on

98
00:04:36,825 --> 00:04:38,171
couplets and leads.

99
00:04:38,771 --> 00:04:39,993
Exam level now.

100
00:04:39,993 --> 00:04:42,282
Match each aid with its feature.

101
00:04:42,282 --> 00:04:43,556
Pause and solve.

102
00:04:48,956 --> 00:04:50,415
The answer is D.

103
00:04:50,415 --> 00:04:53,276
One r, two p, three s, four q.

104
00:04:53,776 --> 00:04:57,180
A herbarium keeps dried,
pressed plants on sheets.

105
00:04:57,180 --> 00:05:00,533
A botanical garden grows
living plants with labels.

106
00:05:01,033 --> 00:05:03,282
A museum pins insects in boxes.

107
00:05:03,282 --> 00:05:07,081
And a key is built from
couplets of opposite characters.

108
00:05:07,681 --> 00:05:09,142
Here is your recap.

109
00:05:09,142 --> 00:05:10,644
Screenshot this card.

110
00:05:13,844 --> 00:05:16,681
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:05:17,081 --> 00:05:17,981
One.

112
00:05:17,577 --> 00:05:20,173
What does a herbarium sheet label carry?

113
00:05:21,073 --> 00:05:21,973
Two.

114
00:05:21,572 --> 00:05:24,932
How is a botanical garden
different from a herbarium?

115
00:05:25,832 --> 00:05:26,732
Three.

116
00:05:26,387 --> 00:05:29,526
What is the difference
between a lead and a couplet?

117
00:05:30,626 --> 00:05:34,212
Next time: bacteria that
live in a boiling hot spring.

118
00:05:34,212 --> 00:05:37,906
Are they even the same kind
of life as ordinary bacteria?

119
00:05:37,906 --> 00:05:40,539
We find out in the three domain system.
